"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[45656],{345656:e=>{e.exports="# SQL Dialect Conversion — SELECT, DML, Window Functions\n\nSource dialect patterns for SELECT, DML, CTEs, window functions, and cursor anti-pattern.\n\n## MSSQL\n\n### SELECT patterns\n\n| T-SQL                          | DBSQL                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| ------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SELECT TOP 10 *`              | `SELECT * ... LIMIT 10`                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `SELECT TOP 10 * ORDER BY col` | `SELECT * ORDER BY col LIMIT 10`                                                                                                                                                                                                              |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `SELECT TOP 10 PERCENT *`      | Use `TABLESAMPLE (10 PERCENT)` for approximate sampling. For exact percentage: `SELECT * FROM (SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY 1) AS _rn, COUNT(*) OVER () AS _total FROM t) AS _tmp WHERE _tmp._rn <= CAST(_tmp._total * 0.10 AS INT)` | `TABLESAMPLE` is the idiomatic choice for sampling. Exact percentage requires a derived table — DBSQL LIMIT does not accept subqueries. Derived tables require an alias (`AS _tmp`). `ORDER BY (SELECT NULL)` is T-SQL-only; use `ORDER BY 1` or a real column. |\n| `PIVOT`                        | `PIVOT (SUM(...) FOR col IN ('Q1','Q2'))`                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Supported; change bracket-quoted `[Q1]` → single-quoted `'Q1'`; for dynamic pivot columns use conditional aggregation                                                                                                                                           |\n| `FOR XML PATH('')`             | `ARRAY_JOIN(COLLECT_LIST(col), sep)` or `STRING_AGG(col, sep)`                                                                                                                                                                                | `GROUP_CONCAT` does not exist in DBSQL; use `STRING_AGG` (Databricks Runtime 11.3+) or `ARRAY_JOIN(COLLECT_LIST(...), sep)`                                                                                                                                     |\n\n### Recursive CTEs\n\nT-SQL recursive CTEs (`WITH cte AS (... UNION ALL SELECT ... FROM cte ...)`) are supported in DBSQL with identical syntax.\n\n> For DBSQL recursive CTE syntax and depth limits, load `skills/writing-sql/recursive-ctes.md`.\n\n### Cursors (anti-pattern)\n\n**Cursors are an anti-pattern in Databricks.** Databricks is optimized for parallel, set-based operations. Row-by-row processing eliminates parallelism benefits.\n\nRewrite as set-based operations wherever possible:\n\n```sql\n-- Instead of a cursor updating row-by-row:\nUPDATE other_table SET processed = 1\nWHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM source_table);\n\n-- Running totals — window function instead of cursor:\nSELECT date, amount,\n       SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY date) AS running_total\nFROM transactions;\n```\n\n> For literal cursor translation when a set-based rewrite is not feasible, see `skills/migration-code-conversion/references/tsql/04-procedures-variables.md`. Cursor syntax is supported in Databricks Serverless SQL via SQL scripting.\n\n### String operations\n\n| T-SQL               | DBSQL                                                | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `string1 + string2` | `CONCAT(string1, string2)` or `string1 \\|\\| string2` | T-SQL `+` propagates NULL; DBSQL `CONCAT` and `\\|\\|` also propagate NULL — safe substitution. **Note**: T-SQL `CONCAT()` (multi-arg) silently swallows NULLs; DBSQL `CONCAT` propagates them. See `00-quirks.md` CONCAT section for details and the `CONCAT_WS` fix. |\n| `REPLICATE(str, n)` | `REPEAT(str, n)`                                     |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `LEN(str)`          | `LENGTH(str)`                                        |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n\n### DML patterns\n\n| T-SQL                                         | DBSQL                                              | Notes                                                              |\n| --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `INSERT INTO ... SELECT ... WHERE NOT EXISTS` | `INSERT INTO ... SELECT ... WHERE NOT EXISTS`      | Same                                                               |\n| `UPDATE FROM JOIN`                            | `MERGE INTO` or `UPDATE ... SET col = (subquery)`  | DBSQL UPDATE does not support FROM JOIN syntax — see example below |\n| `DELETE FROM t FROM t JOIN s`                 | `DELETE FROM t WHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM s ...)` | No DELETE...FROM...JOIN                                            |\n| `TRUNCATE TABLE t`                            | `TRUNCATE TABLE t`                                 | Same                                                               |\n| `SELECT INTO #temp`                           | `CREATE TEMP TABLE staging_data AS SELECT ...`     |                                                                    |\n\n**UPDATE FROM JOIN — example:**\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nUPDATE t\nSET t.status = s.new_status\nFROM orders AS t\nJOIN order_updates AS s ON t.order_id = s.order_id;\n\n-- DBSQL option 1: correlated subquery (no join syntax in UPDATE)\nUPDATE orders\nSET status = (SELECT new_status FROM order_updates WHERE order_updates.order_id = orders.order_id)\nWHERE order_id IN (SELECT order_id FROM order_updates);\n\n-- DBSQL option 2: MERGE (preferred when updating many columns or needing WHEN NOT MATCHED)\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders AS t\nUSING my_catalog.my_schema.order_updates AS s ON t.order_id = s.order_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET status = s.new_status;\n```\n\n### LOG — argument reversal in 2-arg form (behavioral drift)\n\n`LOG(x)` (1-arg, natural log) is identical in both dialects — no change needed.\n\n`LOG(x, base)` (2-arg) has **reversed arguments** in DBSQL:\n\n| T-SQL          | DBSQL          |\n| -------------- | -------------- |\n| `LOG(x, base)` | `LOG(base, x)` |\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL: log base 10 of 100 → 2\nSELECT LOG(100, 10)\n\n-- DBSQL (WRONG — computes log base 100 of 10)\nSELECT LOG(100, 10)\n\n-- DBSQL (CORRECT — arguments reversed)\nSELECT LOG(10, 100)\n```\n\nAlways check every two-argument `LOG()` call and swap the arguments.\n\n### Identical functions (no translation needed)\n\n`SUBSTRING`, `LEFT`, `RIGHT`, `TRIM`, `LTRIM`, `RTRIM`, `REPLACE`, `REVERSE`, `UPPER`, `LOWER`, `ABS`, `FLOOR`, `ROUND`, `SQRT`, `POWER`, `SIGN`, `RAND`, `LOG`, `LOG10`, `EXP`, `PI`, `COUNT`, `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `UNION`, `EXCEPT`, `INTERSECT`\n\nNote: `LOG` is listed here for the 1-arg form only. See the `LOG` drift section above for the 2-arg form.\n\n**Warning — string functions on numeric columns**: `RIGHT`, `LEFT`, and `SUBSTRING` behave identically to T-SQL only when the input column is already a STRING. T-SQL implicitly casts numeric columns (INT, BIGINT, DECIMAL) to string before applying these functions; Databricks SQL does not. When the source column is a numeric type, emit an explicit `CAST(col AS STRING)` first:\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL (calendarID is INT — implicit cast to string)\nSELECT RIGHT(calendarID, 4) FROM t\n\n-- DBSQL (WRONG — type error without explicit cast)\nSELECT RIGHT(calendarID, 4) FROM t\n\n-- DBSQL (CORRECT)\nSELECT RIGHT(CAST(calendarID AS STRING), 4) FROM t\n```\n\n### SELECT TOP N with aggregate functions\n\n| SELECT clause                                                                            | What `TOP N` does                                                                                       | Translation                                                        |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| ONLY aggregate functions (`COUNT(*)`, `SUM(col)`, `MAX(col)` — no non-aggregate columns) | Entire table collapses to 1 row regardless of N. `TOP N` and `ORDER BY <aggregate>` are both redundant. | Remove `TOP N` and `ORDER BY` entirely. Do NOT add `LIMIT`.        |\n| Non-aggregate columns alongside aggregates (e.g. `category, COUNT(*)`)                   | `TOP N` limits rows of a grouped result — `GROUP BY` is almost certainly missing from the source.       | Add `GROUP BY` on all non-aggregate columns, then apply `LIMIT N`. |\n| No aggregate functions (`SELECT TOP N *`, `SELECT TOP N col1, col2`)                     | Standard row limit.                                                                                     | Translate directly with `LIMIT N`.                                 |\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL: scalar aggregate (returns exactly 1 row regardless of TOP 100)\nSELECT TOP 100 COUNT(*) FROM store_sales, household_demographics, time_dim, store WHERE ...\nORDER BY COUNT(*);\n\n-- DBSQL (correct — TOP and ORDER BY removed; scalar aggregate always returns 1 row)\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM store_sales, household_demographics, time_dim, store WHERE ...;\n-- TOP N removed — scalar aggregate always returns exactly 1 row\n\n-- T-SQL: mixed aggregate + non-aggregate (GROUP BY likely missing)\nSELECT TOP 10 category, COUNT(*) FROM t ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC;\n\n-- DBSQL (add GROUP BY, apply LIMIT)\nSELECT category, COUNT(*) FROM t\nGROUP BY category\nORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n-- GROUP BY added — verify intent matches source\n```\n\n### System catalog and metadata queries\n\nT-SQL system catalogs (`sys.*`, `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.*`, `sys.dm_exec_*`) have no direct DBSQL equivalents. Migrate to Unity Catalog information schema or `SHOW` commands:\n\n| T-SQL                                                             | DBSQL equivalent                                                                                                              |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SELECT * FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES`                         | `SHOW TABLES IN catalog.schema` or `SELECT * FROM system.information_schema.tables`                                           |\n| `SELECT * FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS WHERE TABLE_NAME = 't'` | `DESCRIBE TABLE catalog.schema.t` or `SELECT * FROM system.information_schema.columns WHERE table_name = 't'`                 |\n| `SELECT * FROM sys.objects WHERE type = 'U'`                      | `SHOW TABLES` / `SELECT * FROM system.information_schema.tables WHERE table_type = 'BASE TABLE'`                              |\n| `SELECT * FROM sys.columns WHERE object_id = OBJECT_ID('dbo.t')`  | `DESCRIBE TABLE catalog.schema.t`                                                                                             |\n| `SELECT * FROM sys.indexes`                                       | No equivalent — Delta does not use traditional indexes (Z-ORDER and liquid clustering are not queryable as index metadata)    |\n| `sys.dm_exec_requests` / `sys.dm_exec_sessions`                   | No equivalent — use Databricks monitoring UI or `SHOW RUNNING JOBS`                                                           |\n| `OBJECT_ID('dbo.tablename')`                                      | No equivalent — use `SELECT count(*) FROM system.information_schema.tables WHERE table_name = 'tablename'` to check existence |\n\nIf the source uses system catalog queries to enumerate objects dynamically (e.g., to build dynamic SQL), consider whether the pattern can be replaced with a fixed-schema approach or a Python notebook cell using the Databricks SDK.\n\n## SYNAPSE\n\nSame as MSSQL plus:\n\n**PolyBase external tables → managed Delta tables in Unity Catalog:**\n\nSynapse PolyBase external tables read directly from cloud storage. In Databricks, migrate to a **managed Delta table** in Unity Catalog and load data via `COPY INTO` from a UC external location or volume.\n\n> For full `COPY INTO`, external location, and volume syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/foreign-tables.md`.\n\n```sql\n-- Synapse PolyBase (reads parquet directly from storage)\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE ext_sales (...)\nWITH (LOCATION = '/data/sales/', DATA_SOURCE = my_storage, FILE_FORMAT = parquet_format);\n\n-- DBSQL: managed Delta table in Unity Catalog\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.ext_sales (...);\n\n-- Load from cloud storage via a UC external location\nCOPY INTO my_catalog.my_schema.ext_sales\nFROM 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/data/sales/'\nFILEFORMAT = PARQUET;\n```\n"}}]);
